{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"schematic-gleesome-ballpython","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context\n\nThe prior slices establish a bounded structural index and expose Terraform grounding to both review pipelines [rebar:depraved-classless-rooster]. This final slice adds optional independent corroboration from HashiCorp's `terraform-config-inspect` when that executable is already available on `PATH`. It gives an LLM a second implementation of narrow structural facts without turning Rebar into a Terraform validator or running Terraform itself.\n\nThe audited upstream basis is HashiCorp `terraform-config-inspect` commit `2fb54c236733ee65ee877105d595c124c993c64d`. Its shallow loader intentionally extracts stable top-level metadata and evaluates expressions with a nil context; it does not register Terraform filesystem functions, download modules, load providers, or execute Terraform.\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd `src/rebar/grounding/terraform_corroborator.py` and expose `corroborate_diagnostic(module, diagnostic, subject, expected=None)` through the established Terraform-only Pass-1 and Pass-2 sessions.\n\n### Closed diagnostic contract\n\nThe accepted `diagnostic` values are:\n\n- `declaration_present` for a named input variable, output, managed resource, data resource, module call, or required provider.\n- `module_source_equals` for a named module call whose literal source equals the normalized expected source.\n- `required_provider_present` for a named provider requirement.\n\nA schema-valid upstream entry that exactly satisfies the query returns `match` at T1. A missing entry, a non-equal source, a diagnostic claiming absence, or any unsupported/computed semantic returns `abstain`; silence from the upstream tool never proves correctness or absence. The adapter returns only normalized names, structural classifications, source positions, and hashes. It does not return descriptions, defaults, version-constraint payloads, diagnostic bodies, or other literal values present in upstream JSON.\n\n### Executable and snapshot boundary\n\n- Resolve only the exact basename `terraform-config-inspect` from the inherited `PATH`, canonicalize it once, and reject a non-regular/non-executable candidate or one contained by the client repository or temporary snapshot. Invoke that absolute path only; fixed arguments are `--json <snapshot>`. Never pass `--after-init`, `--stack`, repository-controlled arguments, or shell text.\n- Copy the immutable, already-approved whole-module bytes from the structural session into a fresh temporary directory. Include only regular `.tf` and `.tf.json` files, recreate no symlinks, make the snapshot read-only before launch, and set the child working directory to it. The repository path itself is never passed to the child.\n- Launch without a shell, close stdin, start a distinct process group/session, and provide an allowlisted environment containing only locale plus OS-required process variables and temporary `HOME`/`TMPDIR` roots. Strip Terraform/OpenTofu, provider/cloud credential, proxy, plugin, CLI-config, and repository variables.\n- Share the existing 60-second grounding deadline. Cap stdout at 4 MiB and stderr at 64 KiB, terminate then kill the complete process group on timeout/overflow/cancellation, wait for reaping on every exit path, and always delete the snapshot.\n- Hash the executable before and after invocation. Any change abstains. The receipt records backend name, canonical binary path, SHA-256, discoverable version or `unknown`, fixed invocation profile, snapshot digest, normalized query, outcome, and result digest. Child stdout/stderr and environment values are never logged.\n\n### Output contract and failure matrix\n\nValidate stdout against a Rebar-owned schema for the audited upstream JSON fields. All reported filenames must normalize inside the copied snapshot and correspond to copied files. Unknown/malformed shapes, upstream diagnostics, a nonzero exit, output overflow, path mismatch, binary replacement, or version/schema skew return a closed abstention with no partial facts.\n\nAbsence from otherwise valid output also abstains. The corroborator may strengthen a positively matching Pass-1 fact or independently support Pass-2 verification; it cannot originate a finding, veto the ordinary review decision, or convert an abstention into negative evidence.\n\n### Live upstream contract canary\n\nExtend the optionality workflow with a separate, gating canary that installs the exact upstream commit using `go install github.com/hashicorp/terraform-config-inspect@2fb54c236733ee65ee877105d595c124c993c64d`, places only that binary on the test `PATH`, and runs the real adapter over a fixture containing a variable, output, resource, data source, module call, and provider requirement. The test asserts exact positive matches, absence-as-abstention, schema acceptance, receipt provenance, and cleanup. A documented local command runs the identical canary; normal unit tests remain network-free and use an executable fixture at the irreducible process boundary.\n\n## Positive rationale\n\nHashiCorp's deliberately shallow metadata extractor is a narrow authoritative cross-check whose behavior fits Rebar's evidence goal. A minimal copied snapshot and fixed process contract prevent repository configuration, plugins, cached modules, and ambient credentials from affecting the run. Match-only semantics preserve the fail-open boundary where the upstream tool is incomplete.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nTerraform/OpenTofu execution; `terraform validate/plan/init/get`; provider or repository plugin execution; TFLint, Trivy, tfparse, terraform-ls, or terraform-docs execution; module fetching; `--after-init`; stacks; arbitrary upstream diagnostics; absence claims; and semantic expression evaluation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Unit tests over the production adapter prove the exact three-value diagnostic enum and exact-match T1 outcomes for every supported declaration class; nearest missing, unequal, absence-claim, and computed controls all return `abstain`.\n- [ ] Parent/child subprocess tests capture the real argv, cwd, stdin, environment, permissions, and copied bytes, proving only the canonical `terraform-config-inspect --json <snapshot>` process can launch and no repository path, symlink, credential/config variable, or forbidden executable reaches it.\n- [ ] Timeout, output-overflow, cancellation, nonzero-exit, and parser-failure fault tests observe complete process-group reaping and snapshot removal before return; a clean retry then succeeds.\n- [ ] Contract tests feed production-shaped JSON through the real schema consumer and prove diagnostics, unknown/malformed fields, escaping filenames, changed binary digests, and version/schema skew yield one redacted abstention with no partial facts.\n- [ ] Receipt tests assert the executable and snapshot provenance fields while byte-scanning results, receipts, logs, and exception text to prove fixture literals, credentials, stdout, stderr, and environment values do not escape.\n- [ ] Terraform-routed plan-review and code-review Pass 1/Pass 2 advertise `corroborate_diagnostic`; non-IaC and missing-extra sessions do not, and corroborator abstention leaves the ordinary decision path unchanged.\n- [ ] The optionality workflow executes the real adapter against the exact audited upstream commit and fails on contract drift; `docs/grounding.md` documents the identical opt-in local canary command.\n- [ ] ADR 0111 records the executable trust boundary, audited commit, match-only semantics, and explicit prohibition on every other Terraform-related executable.\n\n## Testing\n\nTest contract card:\n\n- Authoritative contract: REB-640, HashiCorp terraform-config-inspect README/JSON contract at commit `2fb54c236733ee65ee877105d595c124c993c64d`, and the prerequisite tool-session contract [rebar:depraved-classless-rooster].\n- Preconditions: an immutable indexed module; executable fixtures for success, skew, secret-bearing output, overflow, hang, signal-resistant child, and self-replacement; plus the pinned real upstream binary in the canary lane.\n- Production path: real session snapshot, discovery, launcher, bounded capture, schema parser, receipt, and workflow tool routing. Unit tests replace only the executable at the process boundary.\n- Tiers: in-process schema/unit tests; real parent/child subprocess tests for isolation and cleanup; workflow integration for visibility; live upstream contract canary for OSS compatibility.\n- Oracles: exact outcome/tier/reason, captured child contract, process nonexistence after return, removed snapshot, successful retry, result/receipt field equality, and byte-level non-disclosure.\n- Negative controls: exact positive fixture matches; the nearest absent/non-equal fact abstains; non-Terraform routes expose no tool.\n- CI: `make test` plus the gating pinned-upstream optionality canary.\n\n## Verification\n\n`env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/grounding/test_terraform_corroborator.py tests/unit/workflow/test_terraform_plan_review_tools.py tests/unit/workflow/test_terraform_code_review_tools.py`","id":"c6ce-4d8d-1c12-47cc","parent_id":"a374-849c-c8f2-4234","priority":3,"tags":["terraform","grounding","corroboration","security","optional"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Corroborate Terraform diagnostics with terraform-config-inspect"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788470032489544433,"uuid":"4e71baa6-557e-4a4a-962e-5fd849772051"}